# Line endings are LF everywhere, working tree included: development happens on
# Windows and CI on Linux, and without this the tree is platform-dependent.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The distribution tarball carries consumption, not development. This file is
# per-variant because what counts as "development" is: a package that ships
# configuration distributes the very .dist files a library hides.
/.github/                export-ignore
/.githooks/              export-ignore
/.rak200                 export-ignore
/.gitmodules             export-ignore
/tests/                  export-ignore
/docs/                   export-ignore
/.editorconfig           export-ignore
/.gitattributes          export-ignore
/.gitignore              export-ignore
/.git-blame-ignore-revs  export-ignore
/.coverage-floor         export-ignore
/CLAUDE.md               export-ignore
/ROADMAP.md              export-ignore
/ARCHITECTURE.md         export-ignore

# A configuration package still has development of its own, and this is where the
# two lists part company. `phpunit.xml` names *this* package's tests and its own
# src/; shipping it hands a consumer a config that can only fail. Release plumbing
# is the same kind of thing — it describes how this repository cuts a tag, and
# means nothing inside a vendor/ directory.
#
# These lines arrived when the package acquired a suite. Before that it had no
# development to separate, which is why a config variant looked for a while like a
# variant that exports everything.
/phpunit.xml                    export-ignore
/release-please-config.json     export-ignore
/.release-please-manifest.json  export-ignore

# Deliberately NOT export-ignored here, and this is the whole reason the file is
# per-variant: in a configuration package the .dist files and CONVENTIONS.md are
# the product. The `php` variant hides exactly what this one must ship. `src/` and
# `bin/` join them: a config package that ships an executable ships the code behind
# it, and Composer's bin proxy autoloads out of the same tarball.
